<h2>The allegation: registration built on paper</h2>
<p>The prosecution case is that the applicant forged documents to register his firm in Uttarakhand, the registration being the gateway to input tax credit benefits under the State&rsquo;s tax regime. The investigation ended in a chargesheet under the cheating and forgery family of offences — Sections 420, 467, 468 and 471 IPC — with Section 467 (forgery of valuable security) carrying a maximum punishment extending to imprisonment for life.</p>

<h2>The tax-offence argument, and why it failed</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>Applicant&rsquo;s framing</h3>
    <p>At worst, this is wrongful availment of ITC under Section 58 of the Uttarakhand VAT Act, 2005 — a first offence inviting a fine of up to Rs 2,000. A regulatory infraction should not become a forgery prosecution.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>Court&rsquo;s answer</h3>
    <p>Section 58 addresses the wrongful taking of credit. The chargesheet alleges something anterior and different: the creation of false documents to obtain the registration itself (para 6). The special statute does not absorb the IPC forgery offences on these facts.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>The distinction matters well beyond this case. GST-era jurisprudence wrestles with the same boundary: when is a tax wrong confined to the fiscal statute&rsquo;s penalty scheme, and when does the underlying conduct — fabricated invoices, forged registrations, impersonated firms — independently constitute IPC (now BNS) offences? This order takes the orthodox line: fabrication of documents is forgery, whatever the fiscal motive.</p>

<h2>The dormant application problem</h2>
<div class="note">
<p>The court added a second, procedural ground (para 8): the application had been pending since 2023 without interim protection, and in all that time the applicant was neither arrested nor did he surrender. If the investigating machinery had not moved against him for years, there was no imminent apprehension of arrest for anticipatory bail to guard against; and an accused cannot park an ABA indefinitely as insurance while the trial court&rsquo;s process goes unanswered.</p>
</div>

<h2>What was directed</h2>
<p>The application was rejected (para 8), with a direction to surrender before the trial court within fifteen days (para 9). The trial court is to decide any bail application expeditiously and uninfluenced by the High Court&rsquo;s observations — the standard formula that preserves the applicant&rsquo;s regular-bail remedies intact.</p>

<h2>Takeaways</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>Economic-offence ABAs are won or lost on characterisation: if the conduct can fairly be described as document fabrication, expect courts to treat it as forgery with the gravity that follows, not as a compoundable fiscal slip.</li>
  <li>Seriousness is measured by the maximum sentence of the charged offence — here, up to life under Section 467 IPC — not by the modest penalty of the overlapping special statute.</li>
  <li>A long-pending ABA with no interim order can itself become a reason for rejection; applications should be prosecuted diligently or withdrawn in favour of surrender and regular bail.</li>
  <li>A surrender direction with an expeditious-decision rider is often the practical endgame in old chargesheeted cases: the real battle shifts to the regular bail hearing before the trial court.</li>
</ul>
</div>
